**Theorem 4.4 (ordered monomorphism occurrence injection).** Fix, for each death $d$, bijective enumerations of the source and target occurrences with death $d$, indexed from zero and sorted by increasing birth. Repeated occurrences are separately indexed; choose their tie order once. An actual componentwise injective natural morphism induces an injection from source occurrences to target occurrences that preserves death and the within-class ordinal, and whose target birth is at most the source birth. This assertion includes essential death $d=\infty$ and empty classes.

**Proof.** Given $b<d$, take the maximum of $b$ and all endpoints from either family strictly below $d$. It is a finite real sample $t<d$, even when $d=\infty$. The monomorphism window $(t,d]$ isolates precisely the death-$d$ class, giving the source-to-target cumulative birth-count bound. For a source occurrence of ordinal $k$, its first $k+1$ class occurrences are all born by its birth. Consequently the target has at least $k+1$ eligible class occurrences. Match to its ordinal $k$. If this target occurrence were born too late, every eligible target occurrence would have ordinal less than $k$, contradicting the count bound. The classwise injections combine by the existing equivalence between an occurrence set and the disjoint union of its endpoint fibers.

